Definition: Optical activity is the ability of some substances to rotate the plane of polarization of light passing through them. The rotation is detected with a polarizer and analyzer (Fig. 8.8). Such substances are said to be optically active.

Examples: quartz crystals, cinnabar (HgS), sugar water, insulin and collagen.

Factors: The amount and direction of rotation depend on:
(1) the type of substance;
(2) the concentration of the substance (the amount present in a given quantity of a mixture or solution);
(3) the distance the light travels through it;
(4) the wavelength of light.

Cause: Optical activity occurs due to the asymmetric shape of the molecules, such as being helical. A few millimetre thickness of such crystals rotates the plane of polarization by many degrees.

Sugar solution: It rotates the plane of polarization of the incident light by an angle θ, so the analyzer stops the light only when rotated by θ from the crossed position.

Use: Organic substances such as sugar and tartaric acid show optical rotation in solution; this property is used to determine their concentration in solutions.
